{"nodes": [{"id": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_dead_letters", "label": "Dead letters (operations page)", "file_type": "document", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/dead-letters.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_dead_letter", "label": "DeadLetter", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/dead-letters.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A tell to a stopped ref, a selection that resolved to nothing, a behavior answering unhandled, a singleton with no host. Every dead letter names its recipient, which is what makes one distinguishable from another."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_deadletterqueue", "label": "system.deadLetterQueue", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/dead-letters.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "withDeadLetters is the only way to configure the queue the system uses \u2014 new DeadLetterQueue(system, options) builds a valid object that nothing installs as the capture sink, because there is one sink slot and the system owns it."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_store_axis", "label": "Four stores on one axis: off, metrics, memory, persistent", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/dead-letters.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Every key in this block decides what is retained, which is why they live under actor-ts.dead-letters and not beside the eventual logging and rate-limiting knobs: those decide how loudly a letter is announced, are read on the publish path, and sit downstream of capture so a suppression setting can never quietly make this record incomplete."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_metrics_store_rationale", "label": "Why a metrics-only store exists", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/dead-letters.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Retaining a payload is a different decision from observing a rate: a ring holds a strong reference to every undeliverable message, which is a data-protection question the moment a payload says anything about a person, and a counter is not. You cannot approximate it with a tiny ring, because max-entries must be positive."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_default_publish_only", "label": "What happens by default", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/dead-letters.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Every dead letter is wrapped and published on the event stream, and that is the whole default. The framework does not log them \u2014 DeadLetterRef holds no logger \u2014 so if nothing subscribes the letter is gone, which is fine for a development run and a poor answer to what did we drop last night."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_replay", "label": "Replaying a captured letter", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/dead-letters.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Replay resolves the recipient path again rather than reusing a ref captured at failure time, because the whole point is that the actor has come back at the same address as a new instance. Every outcome is named, so nothing fails quietly."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_replay_alternate_recipient", "label": "Replaying to a different recipient", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/dead-letters.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Only the destination changes \u2014 the sender stays the recorded one; the recorded path is not resolved at all, because redirecting is most useful exactly when the original address is gone for good; and the replay cap still applies, since alternating between two paths would otherwise hand back the unbounded retry loop the cap exists to close."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_poison_same_entry", "label": "Poison messages cannot grow the queue", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/dead-letters.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A replayed message that dead-letters again comes back as the same entry with a higher replayCount; without that, retrying a message the recipient cannot handle would add an entry per attempt while every attempt still looked like a first."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_persistent_durability", "label": "Graceful-shutdown durability, not crash durability", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/dead-letters.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The stream is derived from the system name so two systems sharing a journal keep separate queues; writes are issued as letters arrive and settled twice at shutdown, because teardown itself produces dead letters after the last phase. A hard kill loses the whole outstanding backlog, not just one write, because tell is synchronous and appends are fire-and-forget onto a serialized chain."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_degraded_payload", "label": "Degraded payloads", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/dead-letters.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The tagged-JSON encoder refuses functions, symbols, Promise, weak collections and cycles rather than corrupting them silently, so a persistent queue keeps the provenance instead of losing the letter \u2014 and such an entry cannot be replayed, because there is nothing left to send."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_what_the_queue_does_not_see", "label": "What the queue does not see", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/dead-letters.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A message discarded by a bounded or priority mailbox never becomes a dead letter: the drop seam reports a reason and never the envelope, so there is nothing to capture and the overflow shows up only in actor_mailbox_dropped_total."}], "edges": [{"source": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_dead_letters", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_dead_letter", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/dead-letters.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_dead_letter", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_default_publish_only", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/dead-letters.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_dead_letter", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_deadletterqueue", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/dead-letters.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_deadletterqueue", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_store_axis", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/dead-letters.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_metrics_store_rationale", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_store_axis", "relation": "rationale_for", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/dead-letters.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_deadletterqueue", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_replay", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/dead-letters.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_replay", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_replay_alternate_recipient", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/dead-letters.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_poison_same_entry", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_replay", "relation": "rationale_for", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/operations/dead-letters.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_persistent_durability", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_operations_dead_letters_store_axis", "relation": "rationale_for", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, 